  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nBetween Tuesday and Thursday, the upper level ridge will remain in \nplace across the region, and as a result, heat will persist and even \nslightly increase during this timeframe. Confidence is quite high \nthat we will see a continuation of upper 90s to near 100 degree \nactual temperatures each day during this window, and likely Friday \nas well for a majority of the area. Not only will afternoon \ntemperatures remain within this range for several days, but \novernight lows are also expected to slowly increase through the week \nthanks to warming ground/pavement temperatures. We still suspect \nthat Ozark (and other) sheltered river valleys that experience cool \nair drainage will see some effective overnight heat relief, and that \novernight lows are being over-estimated by a few degrees in these \nareas. However, this may not be the case in places that will not \nhave this benefit, and particularly urban areas like the St. Louis \nmetro. Meanwhile, as alluded to in the short term discussion, \ncurrent NBM forecast dewpoints in the upper 60s to low 70s are \nlikely a few degrees too high, due to the suspected influence of the \nmodel's bias correction. If this is the case, afternoon heat index \nvalues are also being slightly overestimated. \n\nStill, at a minimum, we are now very confident that almost all of \nthe area will meet the 4-day, 100 degree heat index threshold \nrequired for a Heat Advisory. There is also a reasonable potential \n(50%-70%) for more significant heat impacts within the St. Louis \nMetro area and southwest Illinois, where heat index values may \nexceed 105 degrees for 4 days, and overnight lows may only drop to \nnear 80 degrees. \n\nAs such, area-wide heat headlines have been issued from Monday \nthrough Friday, and are distributed as follows:\n\n- FOR ST. LOUIS METRO AREAS and parts of SOUTHWEST IL, an EXTREME \nHEAT WATCH has been issued, due to the potential for more \nsignificant local heat impacts. \n\n- For all other locations in the NWS St. Louis forecast area, a Heat \nAdvisory has been issued.\n\nBy Friday, confidence in the coverage of impactful heat decreases \nslightly, as the upper ridge may begin to shift westward and open \nthe door for a weak cold front and more northwesterly flow aloft.\nConfidence is relatively low that this will provide widespread \nheat relief as there remains relatively high model spread and \nrun-to-run variability, so we have opted to keep the Advisory \nin place through Friday evening. Not only this, but even if this\nslight pattern shift does provide some modest heat relief, our \narea remains favored for the continuation of above average \ntemperatures through the weekend. Finally, this transition also \nallows for the return of modest precipitation chances (20-30%) \nFriday onward, particularly in northeast MO and IL that could be\nmore exposed to northwest-flow shortwaves.",
